+# Nacelle code review — 2026-08-30
+
+This review treats Nacelle as an untrusted-code execution substrate for a browser-local coding agent, not merely as a compatibility library. Findings are ordered by release impact.
+
+## Executive verdict
+
+The breadth is real enough to be useful, but the dominant risks are boundary composition and lifecycle semantics: transport fallback versus request replay, privileged networking versus worker isolation, package installation versus archive trust, and long-lived browser infrastructure versus per-run cleanup. More Node built-ins are lower priority than making those boundaries explicit and testable.
+
+## 1. [BLOCKER] Native-fetch-first fallback can replay non-idempotent requests
+
+**Impact.** A browser fetch may reach the target and still reject locally because the response is CORS-blocked. Falling back to Nacelle+ after that rejection can send POST/PATCH/DELETE twice. For model APIs this means duplicate charges or duplicated tool actions.
+
+**Recommended change.** Select the transport before issuing unsafe cross-origin requests. Use extension-first when a privileged grant exists; only retry GET/HEAD after a native failure. Never infer “not sent” from fetch TypeError.
+
+**Evidence.**
+
