+## 11. [FEATURE] Capability manifest and per-run policy
+
+**Impact.** The agent, project code, npm scripts, and extension currently need different authority. Without a single declarative policy, privileges will accrete through ad-hoc booleans and implicit host behavior.
+
+**Recommended change.** Add a signed/inspectable capability manifest: network origins/methods, secrets, npm scripts, host bridge APIs, persistence, workers, preview ports, and resource budgets. Prompt only on capability deltas.
+
+**Evidence.**
+
+_No single source location; this is an architectural gap inferred from the absence of a cohesive facility._
+
+## 12. [FEATURE] Transactional checkpoints, diff, rollback, and reproducible runs
+
+**Impact.** End-to-end agents need a safe unit of work. A failed install/build should not corrupt the only workspace, and users need to inspect exactly what the agent changed.
+
+**Recommended change.** Execute each agent turn against a snapshot; expose commit/rollback; persist content-addressed checkpoints; record package lock + runtime version + capabilities; export a normal git diff.
+
+**Evidence.**
+
+_No single source location; this is an architectural gap inferred from the absence of a cohesive facility._
+
+## 13. [FEATURE] Compatibility lab based on real repositories
+
+**Impact.** The useful unit is not “built-in module loads”; it is “repo installs, runs, tests, previews, and stops cleanly without patches.”
+
+**Recommended change.** Maintain a public corpus across Vite/React/TS, Vitest/Jest, ESLint, monorepos, package managers, native/WASM packages, and hostile packages. Publish pass/fail, startup time, peak memory, and unsupported semantic reason.
+
+**Evidence.**
+
+_No single source location; this is an architectural gap inferred from the absence of a cohesive facility._
